const PATTERN_ACADEMY_12 = {
  "2d-grid-dp": {
    title: "Pattern 59 -- 2D Grid DP",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Now we're moving from dp[i] to dp[row][col]. This is one of the easiest DP families to recognize once you understand the state. The central idea: the answer for a cell depends on answers for smaller/previous cells.",
        conclusion: "Same skeleton as 1D DP -- just one more dimension." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why Does 2D Grid DP Exist?", dark: true,
        body: "Imagine a matrix:\n\n      c0   c1   c2   c3\nr0    v    v    v    v\nr1    v    v    v    v\nr2    v    v    v    v\nr3    v    v    v    v\n\nYou're moving through the matrix. The answer at (row, col) may depend on (row-1, col) and (row, col-1). So one index isn't enough -- we need dp[row][col]." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "Think: what smaller cells can reach this cell? For a standard grid where you can move RIGHT or DOWN, cell (r,c) can be reached from (r-1,c) (above) or (r,c-1) (left).\n\nTherefore: dp[r][c] = combine(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1]). This is the fundamental Grid DP pattern." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "3. Recognition Triggers",
        intro: "Think 2D Grid DP when you see:",
        items: ["matrix/grid", "rows and columns", "move right/down", "minimum cost path", "maximum score path", "number of paths", "obstacles", "collect maximum points", "reach destination", "cells depend on neighboring cells", "movement is directional"],
        note: "Strong trigger: 'Find the number/minimum/maximum way to reach a cell in a grid.'" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "4. The First Question", tone: "red",
        body: "When you see a grid problem, ask: what directions can I move? If RIGHT/DOWN, dp[r][c] can depend on dp[r-1][c] and dp[r][c-1]. If movement is UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT, the problem may instead be BFS, DFS, or Dijkstra rather than simple DP.",
        conclusion: "Movement rules matter." },
      { type: "text", heading: "5. Pattern 1 -- Unique Paths",
        body: "Robot starts at top-left and can move only right or down. How many ways to reach bottom-right? Example: a 3x3 grid, S -> -> / v v v / -> -> E." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "6-7. Define the State & Base Cases", dark: true,
        body: "dp[r][c] means: number of ways to reach cell (r,c). To reach (r,c): from above, or from left. Therefore: dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1].\n\nBase cases: dp[0][0] = 1. First row: dp[0][c] = 1 (only way is RIGHT RIGHT RIGHT...). First column: dp[r][0] = 1 (only way is DOWN DOWN DOWN...)." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. Java -- Unique Paths", dark: true,
        body: "public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {\n\n    int[][] dp = new int[m][n];\n\n    for (int r = 0; r < m; r++) {\n        dp[r][0] = 1;\n    }\n\n    for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n        dp[0][c] = 1;\n    }\n\n    for (int r = 1; r < m; r++) {\n        for (int c = 1; c < n; c++) {\n            dp[r][c] = dp[r - 1][c] + dp[r][c - 1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[m - 1][n - 1];\n}" },
      { type: "variants", heading: "9. The Most Important Formula -- combine() by goal", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Counting", note: "dp[r][c] = combine(previous cells) with +" },
        { id: "2", title: "Minimum", note: "combine with min" },
        { id: "3", title: "Maximum", note: "combine with max" }
      ], conclusion: "Exactly like 1D DP. The only difference is 1 dimension -> 2 dimensions." },
      { type: "text", heading: "10. Unique Paths With Obstacles",
        body: "Now suppose some cells are blocked: 0 = free, 1 = obstacle. For example:\n0 0 0\n0 1 0\n0 0 0\nThe center cannot be used." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11-12. State & Java -- With Obstacles", dark: true,
        body: "dp[r][c] still means number of valid ways to reach (r,c). If grid[r][c] == obstacle, dp[r][c] = 0. Otherwise: dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1].\n\npublic int uniquePathsWithObstacles(int[][] grid) {\n\n    int m = grid.length;\n    int n = grid[0].length;\n    int[][] dp = new int[m][n];\n\n    if (grid[0][0] == 1) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n\n    dp[0][0] = 1;\n\n    for (int r = 0; r < m; r++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {\n\n            if (grid[r][c] == 1) {\n                dp[r][c] = 0;\n                continue;\n            }\n\n            if (r > 0) {\n                dp[r][c] += dp[r - 1][c];\n            }\n\n            if (c > 0) {\n                dp[r][c] += dp[r][c - 1];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    return dp[m - 1][n - 1];\n}" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13. Why This Is Still DP", tone: "gold",
        body: "The obstacle doesn't change the underlying structure. The state remains (r,c). The obstacle simply makes the state unreachable: dp[r][c] = 0.",
        conclusion: "Constraints often modify transitions rather than changing the entire DP pattern." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14-15. Pattern 2 -- Minimum Path Sum",
        body: "Find the minimum cost path from top-left to bottom-right. Grid:\n1 3 1\n1 5 1\n4 2 1\n\ndp[r][c] means: minimum cost to reach cell (r,c). To reach (r,c): from above, or from left. So: dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1])." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "16. Notice the Pattern", items: [
        { label: "Unique Paths", body: "dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1]" },
        { label: "Minimum Path", body: "dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1])" }
      ], conclusion: "Same structure -- only the aggregation operator changes." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17. General Grid DP Formula", dark: true,
        body: "dp[r][c] = cellValue + combine(valid predecessor states)\n\nwhere combine = + / min / max / OR, depending on the problem." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "18-19. Grid DP and Direction / General Ordering Rule", tone: "red",
        body: "If movement is RIGHT/DOWN, dependencies are UP/LEFT. Iterate top->bottom, left->right, so required states are already computed when you reach dp[r][c].\n\nDon't memorize 'always loop row first.' Instead ask: 'Are all dependencies already computed when I process this state?' If yes, valid order. If not, change iteration direction.",
        conclusion: "This principle applies far beyond grids." },
      { type: "text", heading: "20. What If Movement Is Left/Right?",
        body: "If you can move both left and right, dp[r][c] may depend on another cell that isn't strictly 'earlier.' Simple forward DP may no longer work -- you need to understand whether the state graph is acyclic. This leads to DAG DP, or potentially BFS/Dijkstra depending on the problem." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "21-22. Grid DP vs BFS vs Dijkstra", items: [
        { label: "Grid DP", body: "Use when movement has a natural direction/order, dependencies don't create cycles, you're calculating min/max/count, and state can be processed in topological order. Example: RIGHT/DOWN." },
        { label: "BFS", body: "Use when movement is arbitrary, every move has equal cost, and you need shortest number of moves. Example: UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT." },
        { label: "Dijkstra", body: "If movement has different costs (A->B costs 1, A->C costs 10) and movement can create arbitrary paths, Dijkstra may be appropriate. If movement is strictly RIGHT/DOWN and the graph is acyclic, DP is usually simpler." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "23-24. Grid DP vs DFS + Memoization", dark: true,
        body: "Suppose you define dfs(r,c) and recursively explore valid next cells. If states repeat, you can memoize memo[r][c] -- that's still DP: DFS + memoization = Top-down Grid DP.\n\nint dfs(int r, int c) {\n\n    if (r == m - 1 && c == n - 1) {\n        return grid[r][c];\n    }\n\n    if (memo[r][c] != -1) {\n        return memo[r][c];\n    }\n\n    int down = dfs(r + 1, c);\n    int right = dfs(r, c + 1);\n\n    return memo[r][c] = grid[r][c] + Math.min(down, right);\n}" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "25. Bottom-Up vs Top-Down", items: [
        { label: "Top-down", body: "Start destination/problem -> recursively ask smaller states -> memo." },
        { label: "Bottom-up", body: "Start base -> build toward destination." }
      ], conclusion: "Both represent the same recurrence." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "26-27. Space Optimization & 1D Compression", tone: "gold",
        body: "If dp[r][c] only depends on dp[r-1][c] and dp[r][c-1], you don't need the entire matrix -- use int[] dp = new int[n], where dp[c] represents the current best value for that column.\n\nint[] dp = new int[n];\ndp[0] = grid[0][0];\n\nfor (int c = 1; c < n; c++) {\n    dp[c] = dp[c - 1] + grid[0][c];\n}\n\nfor (int r = 1; r < m; r++) {\n    dp[0] += grid[r][0];\n\n    for (int c = 1; c < n; c++) {\n        dp[c] = grid[r][c] + Math.min(dp[c], dp[c - 1]);\n    }\n}\n\nreturn dp[n - 1];",
        conclusion: "dp[c] before update = previous row; after update = current row." },
      { type: "text", heading: "28. Why In-Place Update Works",
        body: "At dp[c] before updating: dp[c] = previous row, and dp[c-1] = current row (already updated this pass). So min(dp[c], dp[c-1]) gives exactly min(top, left). This is a classic space compression pattern." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "29. Recognition: 2D DP -> 1D DP", tone: "red",
        body: "Whenever dp[r][c] depends only on the previous row plus the current row's prefix, ask: can I compress the row dimension? If yes: O(mn) space becomes O(n) space.",
        conclusion: "A very common optimization interviewers ask about." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "30. Grid DP + Obstacles -- sentinel values", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Counting", note: "dp = 0 for blocked/impossible" },
        { id: "2", title: "Minimum", note: "dp = INF for blocked/impossible" },
        { id: "3", title: "Maximum", note: "dp = -INF for blocked/impossible" }
      ], conclusion: "The sentinel depends on the problem." },
      { type: "text", heading: "31-32. Grid DP + Maximum Score & Boolean",
        body: "Maximum score: each cell contains points, maximize score reaching destination -> dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + max(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1]).\n\nReachability: 'Can I reach the destination?' -> dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] || dp[r][c-1]. Same state, different aggregation." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "33. The Four Main Grid DP Operators", items: [
        { label: "Number of ways", body: "+" },
        { label: "Minimum cost", body: "min" },
        { label: "Maximum score", body: "max" },
        { label: "Reachable?", body: "OR" }
      ], conclusion: "Exactly the same principle you learned in 1D DP." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "34-35. Grid DP With More Directions & Acyclic Dependencies", tone: "red",
        body: "If allowed movement is up/down/left/right, dependencies can form cycles (A->B->A). Simple DP fails because there is no natural topological order -- you need BFS, DFS, Dijkstra, or a DAG transformation instead.\n\nDP works naturally when states can be ordered (A -> B -> C). If A->B, B->C, C->A creates cycles, simple bottom-up DP isn't directly applicable. Memoized DFS still works if the state graph is effectively acyclic under the chosen transitions, but genuine cyclic dependencies require other techniques.",
        conclusion: "One of the most important 'when NOT to use DP' rules." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "36. Grid DP vs BFS Recognition", items: [
        { label: "How many ways?", body: "DP, if movement is directional/acyclic." },
        { label: "Minimum number of steps?", body: "BFS, if every move has equal cost." },
        { label: "Minimum weighted cost?", body: "Dijkstra, if arbitrary weighted movement exists." },
        { label: "Max/min path, only right/down?", body: "DP." }
      ]},
      { type: "variants", heading: "37-40. Common Mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Assuming the recurrence", note: "Using dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1] without checking whether the moves are actually up/down/left/right. The recurrence comes from the movement rules -- never memorize it without deriving the predecessors." },
        { id: "2", title: "Incorrect boundary handling", note: "For r=0 there is no r-1; for c=0 there is no c-1. Use boundary initialization or conditional checks." },
        { id: "3", title: "Forgetting the start cell", note: "For counting: dp[0][0] = 1. For cost: dp[0][0] = grid[0][0]. The starting state's meaning determines the initialization." },
        { id: "4", title: "Wrong iteration order", note: "If dp[r][c] depends on dp[r-1][c] and dp[r][c-1], process r increasing, c increasing. If dependencies point another direction, the order must change." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "41. The Interview Derivation", tone: "gold",
        body: "'Find minimum cost path from top-left to bottom-right, moving only right or down.' Strong answer: 'I'll define dp[r][c] as the minimum cost to reach cell (r,c). Since the only predecessors are the cell above and the cell to the left, the recurrence is the current cell cost plus the minimum of those two states.' Then: dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1]). Then mention: 'Because each state only depends on the previous row and current row's previous column, I can reduce space from O(mn) to O(n).'",
        conclusion: "That's a very good interview response." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "42. The Unified DP Picture", dark: true,
        body: "1D:            dp[i]\nState Machine: dp[i][state]\nPrefix:        dp[i] + split j\nGrid:          dp[r][c]\n\nAll are the same fundamental idea: define a state that completely describes the smaller problem, identify predecessor states, and combine them." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "43. Grid DP Recognition Cheat Sheet", items: [
        { label: "Right + Down", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Number of paths", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Minimum path cost", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Maximum path score", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Obstacles", body: "Grid DP + constraints" },
        { label: "Reachability", body: "Boolean Grid DP" },
        { label: "4-direction shortest path", body: "Usually BFS" },
        { label: "Weighted 4-direction path", body: "Usually Dijkstra" },
        { label: "Repeated DFS states", body: "DFS + Memoization" },
        { label: "Previous row/current column only", body: "1D space optimization" },
        { label: "Arbitrary cycles", body: "Usually not simple DP" }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "44. Master Template", dark: true,
        body: "for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n    for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n\n        if (blocked(r, c)) {\n            dp[r][c] = IMPOSSIBLE;\n            continue;\n        }\n\n        dp[r][c] = combine(predecessorStates, grid[r][c]);\n    }\n}\n\nYour real job: 1. What does dp[r][c] mean? 2. Which cells can reach (r,c)? 3. What is the aggregation? 4. What are the boundaries? 5. What is the iteration order? 6. Can space be compressed?" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 6 progress",
        items: [{ label: "54. 1D DP", done: true }, { label: "55. Take / Skip DP", done: true },
          { label: "56. Linear DP", done: true }, { label: "57. State Machine DP", done: true },
          { label: "58. Prefix DP", done: true }, { label: "59. 2D Grid DP", done: true },
          { label: "60. Grid Path DP", done: false }, { label: "61. Constraint Grid DP", done: false },
          { label: "62. 0/1 Knapsack", done: false }, { label: "63. Unbounded Knapsack", done: false },
          { label: "64. Subset Sum", done: false }, { label: "65. Partition DP", done: false },
          { label: "66. Target Sum", done: false }, { label: "67. Coin Change", done: false },
          { label: "68. LCS", done: false }, { label: "69. LIS", done: false },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: false }, { label: "71. Palindrome DP", done: false },
          { label: "72. String DP", done: false }, { label: "73. Interval DP", done: false },
          { label: "74. Matrix Chain", done: false }, { label: "75. Burst Balloons", done: false },
          { label: "76. Stock DP", done: false }, { label: "77. Counting DP", done: false },
          { label: "78. Tree DP", done: false }, { label: "79. DAG DP", done: false },
          { label: "80. Bitmask DP", done: false }, { label: "81. Digit DP", done: false },
          { label: "82. DP + Binary Search", done: false }, { label: "83. DP + Monotonic Queue", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Grid Path DP",
        points: ["A slightly deeper version of Grid DP: dp[cell] vs dp[state of reaching cell]", "Minimum/maximum paths, multiple movement directions, path constraints, obstacles, path reconstruction", "Space optimization, and when Grid DP should become BFS/Dijkstra instead"] }
    ]
  },
  "grid-path-dp": {
    title: "Pattern 60 -- Grid Path DP",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "Basic Grid DP asked: 'How many ways can I reach this cell?' Grid Path DP expands that to: 'Among all valid paths to this cell, what is the best/count/possible result?' The key move: merge every path that reaches the same cell into one DP state -- you never enumerate paths one by one.",
        conclusion: "State merging is the entire optimization." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. Why Does Grid Path DP Exist?", dark: true,
        body: "1 3 1\n1 5 1\n4 2 1\n\nStart (0,0), destination (2,2), moves RIGHT/DOWN only. Many different paths reach (2,2). Enumerating all of them is wasteful -- instead we merge every path that reaches a given cell into a single dp[r][c] value." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "2. Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "(0,0)\n  |\n(1,0) -> (1,1)\n  |         |\n(2,0) -> (2,1) -> (2,2)\n\nMany paths may reach (2,1). Once we know the required info about (2,1), we don't care which exact path produced it. That's state merging." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "3. Recognition Triggers",
        intro: "Think Grid Path DP when you see:",
        items: ["path through matrix", "start -> destination", "move right/down/diagonal", "minimum path", "maximum path", "number of paths", "path score", "collect coins/points", "obstacles", "path constraints", "repeated grid positions"] },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "4. The Fundamental State", dark: true,
        body: "dp[r][c] usually means one of:\n\nminimum cost to reach (r,c)\nmaximum score to reach (r,c)\nnumber of paths to (r,c)\nwhether (r,c) is reachable\n\nThe exact meaning must be explicitly defined -- never left implicit." },
      { type: "variants", heading: "5. Four Main Grid Path DP Types", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Counting", note: "dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1] -- number of paths." },
        { id: "2", title: "Minimum", note: "dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1]) -- minimum path cost." },
        { id: "3", title: "Maximum", note: "dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + max(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1]) -- maximum path score." },
        { id: "4", title: "Feasibility", note: "dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] || dp[r][c-1] -- can reach this cell at all?" }
      ], conclusion: "Same state, same skeleton -- only the aggregation operator changes." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "6-9. Pattern A-D Side by Side", items: [
        { label: "A. Minimum Path", body: "dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1])" },
        { label: "B. Maximum Path", body: "dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + max(dp[r-1][c], dp[r][c-1])" },
        { label: "C. Count Paths", body: "dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1] -- not maximizing/minimizing, just counting." },
        { label: "D. Reachability", body: "dp[r][c] = dp[r-1][c] || dp[r][c-1] -- same structure, boolean operator." }
      ], conclusion: "Four different questions, one identical dependency shape." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "10. The Most Important Question", tone: "red",
        body: "Before writing the recurrence, ask: what are the legal predecessors of this cell? RIGHT/DOWN movement -> predecessors are UP and LEFT. RIGHT/DOWN/DIAGONAL movement -> predecessors are UP, LEFT, and UP-LEFT. dp[r][c] must combine all of them.",
        conclusion: "Movement rules directly determine DP dependencies." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Diagonal Movement", dark: true,
        body: "Allowed: RIGHT, DOWN, DIAGONAL\n\ndp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(\n    dp[r-1][c],\n    dp[r][c-1],\n    dp[r-1][c-1]\n)\n\nfor minimum cost. One extra direction, one extra term -- nothing else changes." },
      { type: "text", heading: "12. Variable Jump Length",
        body: "Suppose from (r,c) you may jump up to K cells right. Then dp[r][c] can depend on dp[r][c-1] ... dp[r][c-K]: dp[r][c] = grid[r][c] + min(dp[r][c-k]) for 1 <= k <= K. This is still Grid Path DP, but naively it costs O(K) per cell. Later, DP + Monotonic Queue can optimize some of these transitions to O(1) amortized." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "13. Path Constraints", tone: "red",
        body: "If a restriction only depends on the current cell (e.g. 'skip cells above a limit'), dp[r][c] is still enough. But if the restriction depends on something accumulated along the path -- like 'total sum collected must equal exactly K' -- then (r,c) alone no longer determines the future. You need dp[r][c][sum], or a set/map of reachable sums per cell.",
        conclusion: "If a variable affects future decisions, it belongs in the state." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "14. Example -- Path With Exact Sum", dark: true,
        body: "grid:\n1 2\n3 4\n\nNeed a path with sum 7. 1->2->4 = 7 works. 1->3->4 = 8 does not.\n\nState: dp[r][c][sum] means 'can we reach (r,c) with this running sum?' This is Grid DP fused with a Subset/Target-Sum-style dimension." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "15. Grid DP + State", dark: true,
        body: "Basic:                 dp[r][c]\nWith remaining energy:  dp[r][c][energy]\nWith obstacles removed: dp[r][c][k]\nWith collected keys:    dp[r][c][mask]\nWith time:              dp[r][c][time]\n\nThe grid is just one dimension of the state -- not the whole state." },
      { type: "text", heading: "16. Example -- Eliminate K Obstacles",
        body: "Move top-left to bottom-right, may remove at most K obstacles along the way. Reaching (r,c) with obstaclesUsed = 1 and reaching (r,c) with obstaclesUsed = K are genuinely different situations -- they have different future possibilities. So the state must become dp[r][c][k]: Grid DP + additional state." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "17. But Is It Always DP?", tone: "red",
        body: "If movement allows UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT, the dependency graph has cycles (A -> B -> C -> A is possible). Simple bottom-up DP doesn't work there. For shortest path with equal edge cost, BFS is usually the right tool. For weighted edges, Dijkstra may be needed.",
        conclusion: "Don't force Grid DP onto a cyclic dependency graph." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "18-19. Grid Path DP vs BFS vs Dijkstra", items: [
        { label: "Grid Path DP", body: "Dependencies are directional/acyclic. Example: RIGHT + DOWN. Use for min/max/count where a topological order exists." },
        { label: "BFS", body: "Movement is arbitrary, all moves cost the same, you want minimum number of moves. Example: UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT unweighted." },
        { label: "Dijkstra", body: "Arbitrary movement + different edge/cell costs + minimum total cost. Example: weighted grid graph, need cheapest path overall." }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "20. Grid Path DP vs DFS + Memoization", dark: true,
        body: "If movement only goes right/down, dfs(r,c) has a finite, acyclic dependency graph:\n\nint dfs(int r, int c) {\n    if (r == 0 && c == 0) return grid[0][0];\n    if (memo[r][c] != -1) return memo[r][c];\n\n    int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n    if (r > 0) best = Math.min(best, dfs(r - 1, c));\n    if (c > 0) best = Math.min(best, dfs(r, c - 1));\n\n    return memo[r][c] = grid[r][c] + best;\n}\n\nDFS + memo = Top-down Grid DP." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "21-22. Path Reconstruction",
        intro: "Sometimes you must return the actual path, not just the optimal value. DP handles this with parent pointers:",
        items: [
          "Compute dp[r][c] as usual (minimum cost, maximum score, etc).",
          "At each cell, also store where the optimal value came from: parent[r][c] = UP or parent[r][c] = LEFT.",
          "Example: if dp[1][2] = 6 and dp[2][1] = 10, and dp[2][2] = 7 came from dp[1][2], then parent[2][2] = UP.",
          "Start at the destination, follow parent pointers back to the source, then reverse the collected path."
        ],
        note: "Same idea used in shortest path, sequence reconstruction, LIS reconstruction, and edit distance reconstruction." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "23. Space Optimization vs Path Reconstruction", tone: "gold",
        body: "If you only need the minimum/maximum value, you can aggressively compress dp[r][c] down to dp[c] (O(n) space). But if you also need the actual path, you typically need to retain parent[r][c] in full -- compressing it away usually loses the information required to reconstruct a path.",
        conclusion: "Value only -> compress freely. Value + path -> keep reconstruction info." },
      { type: "text", heading: "24. Obstacles + Path Reconstruction",
        body: "With 0 = open and 1 = blocked, combine dp[r][c] + parent[r][c] as usual, but only ever store a parent pointer when the cell is actually reachable. That lets you reconstruct one valid optimal path even through a grid full of obstacles." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "25-26. Initialization Pitfalls (Max and Min)", tone: "red",
        body: "Maximum path with negative values: don't initialize impossible states to 0 -- a 0 might incorrectly beat a real but negative path sum. Use Integer.MIN_VALUE (or an equivalent negative infinity) instead. Minimum path with impossible states: don't initialize to 0 either -- use a large INF constant, e.g. static final int INF = 1_000_000_000, so an impossible state never wins the min() comparison.",
        conclusion: "Both are classic, easy-to-miss interview bugs." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "27. Counting Paths and Overflow", tone: "gold",
        body: "The number of paths through a large grid can explode combinatorially (2^N or more). A plain int can silently overflow. Depending on the problem, switch to long, or take results modulo a constant such as 1_000_000_007.",
        conclusion: "A very practical, very common interview detail." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "28. Space Optimization -- Look at the Dependency Graph", tone: "gold",
        body: "Basic Grid Path DP is O(rows x cols) space. If each row only depends on the previous row, that drops to O(cols). If each cell depends on only a fixed, small number of nearby cells, sometimes even more compression is possible.",
        conclusion: "Look at the dependency graph, not the matrix dimensions." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "29-30. Grid Path DP Is Really DAG DP", dark: true,
        body: "For RIGHT + DOWN:\n\n(0,0) -> (0,1) -> (0,2)\n  |        |        |\n(1,0) -> (1,1) -> (1,2)\n  |        |        |\n(2,0) -> (2,1) -> (2,2)\n\nThis is a DAG. That's why DP works at all -- iteration order is simply a topological ordering of this DAG. A directional grid is just a structured, special-case DAG." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "31-32. Diagonal DAG vs Four-Direction Cycles", dark: true,
        body: "RIGHT + DOWN + DIAGONAL: still a DAG -- every arrow points forward, so DP still works.\n\nUP + DOWN + LEFT + RIGHT:\n\nA -> B\n^    |\n|    v\nD <- C\n\nCycles exist. Simple bottom-up DP fails; reach for BFS, Dijkstra, DFS+memo (if genuinely acyclic under the actual transitions), or Dijkstra + state." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "33. Recognition Decision Tree",
        intro: "Walk through these four questions in order whenever you see a grid problem:",
        items: [
          "Step 1 -- What is the objective? Count? Min? Max? Reachability? Shortest steps?",
          "Step 2 -- What are the allowed moves? Right/down only? Arbitrary? Weighted?",
          "Step 3 -- Do dependencies form cycles? No -> DP candidate. Yes -> BFS/Dijkstra/other graph algorithm.",
          "Step 4 -- Does the current cell fully describe the future? Yes -> dp[r][c]. No -> add dimensions: dp[r][c][k / sum / mask / energy / time]."
        ]},
      { type: "variants", heading: "34. Example Recognition", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Max coins, only right or down", note: "grid + max + directional -> Grid Path DP." },
        { id: "2", title: "Shortest moves, four directions, unweighted", note: "grid + shortest steps + unweighted + cycles -> BFS." },
        { id: "3", title: "Min cost, four directions, weighted cells", note: "grid + weighted + cycles -> Dijkstra candidate." },
        { id: "4", title: "Max score right/down, but one teleport allowed", note: "(r,c) alone isn't enough -- need (r,c,teleportUsed) -> Grid DP + state." }
      ]},
      { type: "text", heading: "35-36. Grid Path DP + Bitmask / Keys",
        body: "'Visit all special cells while minimizing distance' -> state becomes (row, col, mask) where mask = special cells visited so far: Grid + Bitmask + Shortest Path/DP. 'Grid contains keys and doors, movement depends on collected keys' -> state becomes (r, c, keysMask), because (r,c) alone can't tell you whether a door is currently passable." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "37. The Most Important Principle", tone: "gold",
        body: "State = minimum information needed to determine the future. Basic path -> (r,c). Obstacle elimination -> (r,c,k). Keys -> (r,c,mask). Energy -> (r,c,energy). Time -> (r,c,time).",
        conclusion: "One of the deepest ideas in all of DP -- master this and every 'add a dimension' problem becomes mechanical." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "38. Common Interview Mistake", tone: "red",
        body: "Wrong reasoning: 'It's a grid, so use DP.' Correct reasoning: Grid -> what are the transitions? -> are they acyclic? -> what is the objective? -> what state is required? -> then choose DP / BFS / Dijkstra / DFS / 0-1 BFS.",
        conclusion: "This distinction matters a lot in FAANG-style interviews." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "39. Master Grid Path Template", dark: true,
        body: "state = (r, c)\n\ndp[r][c] = combine(\n    all valid predecessor states\n) + currentContribution\n\nThen ask: Can state be compressed? Can the path be reconstructed? Are there extra constraints? Are there cycles?" },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "40. Grid Path Cheat Sheet", items: [
        { label: "Right/down + count", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Right/down + min", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Right/down + max", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Right/down + obstacles", body: "Grid DP" },
        { label: "Right/down + exact sum", body: "Grid DP + sum state" },
        { label: "Four directions + shortest steps", body: "BFS" },
        { label: "Four directions + weighted cost", body: "Dijkstra" },
        { label: "Four directions + keys", body: "BFS + bitmask" },
        { label: "Four directions + obstacle elimination", body: "BFS + state" },
        { label: "Directional grid + repeated DFS", body: "DFS + memoization" },
        { label: "Visit all special cells", body: "Bitmask + graph/DP" }
      ]},
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 6 progress",
        items: [{ label: "54. 1D DP", done: true }, { label: "55. Take / Skip DP", done: true },
          { label: "56. Linear DP", done: true }, { label: "57. State Machine DP", done: true },
          { label: "58. Prefix DP", done: true }, { label: "59. 2D Grid DP", done: true },
          { label: "60. Grid Path DP", done: true }, { label: "61. Constraint Grid DP", done: false },
          { label: "62. 0/1 Knapsack", done: false }, { label: "63. Unbounded Knapsack", done: false },
          { label: "64. Subset Sum", done: false }, { label: "65. Partition DP", done: false },
          { label: "66. Target Sum", done: false }, { label: "67. Coin Change", done: false },
          { label: "68. LCS", done: false }, { label: "69. LIS", done: false },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: false }, { label: "71. Palindrome DP", done: false },
          { label: "72. String DP", done: false }, { label: "73. Interval DP", done: false },
          { label: "74. Matrix Chain", done: false }, { label: "75. Burst Balloons", done: false },
          { label: "76. Stock DP", done: false }, { label: "77. Counting DP", done: false },
          { label: "78. Tree DP", done: false }, { label: "79. DAG DP", done: false },
          { label: "80. Bitmask DP", done: false }, { label: "81. Digit DP", done: false },
          { label: "82. DP + Binary Search", done: false }, { label: "83. DP + Monotonic Queue", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "Constraint Grid DP",
        points: ["When dp[r][c] stops being enough, and constraints force extra dimensions: dp[r][c][k], dp[r][c][sum], dp[r][c][mask], dp[r][c][energy]", "How to recognize the exact moment a plain grid problem becomes a compound-state problem", "Connecting constraint-driven grid states to BFS + state, bitmask techniques, pruning, and shortest-path algorithms"] }
    ]
  },
  "constraint-grid-dp": {
    title: "Pattern 61 -- Constraint Grid DP",
    sections: [
      { type: "callout", heading: "The core idea", tone: "gold",
        body: "This is the last major Grid DP pattern before Knapsack. The upgrade: in basic Grid DP, (row,col) completely describes the problem state. In Constraint Grid DP, it doesn't -- you must add whatever information affects what you can do next.",
        conclusion: "Position alone is no longer the whole story." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "1. The Core Idea", dark: true,
        body: "Basic: dp[r][c] means 'what is the best/count/possible result at this cell?'\n\nBut suppose: 'you can eliminate at most K obstacles.' Now these are different situations even at the same position:\n\n(r,c, 0 obstacles used)\n(r,c, 1 obstacle used)\n(r,c, 2 obstacles used)\n\nTherefore: dp[r][c][k]." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "2. The Golden Rule", tone: "red",
        body: "If two situations at the same position have different future possibilities, they must be different DP states.",
        conclusion: "Memorize this -- it matters far more than memorizing individual DP problems." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "3. Mental Model", dark: true,
        body: "                position (r,c)\n                      |\n          +-----------+-----------+\n          v           v           v\n       state 0     state 1     state 2\n\nInstead of dp[r][c] you have dp[r][c][state]. The extra state might be: k, sum, energy, mask, time, keys, coupons." },
      { type: "chips", heading: "4. Recognition Triggers",
        intro: "Look for phrases like:",
        items: ["you may use at most K...", "you have K chances...", "you can remove K obstacles", "remaining energy", "remaining moves", "exactly K...", "at most K...", "you have collected...", "keys obtained", "coupon used", "power remaining", "must collect...", "can teleport once", "one special move"],
        note: "These are strong signals that dp[r][c] alone may not be sufficient." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "5. The Most Important Question", tone: "gold",
        body: "When reading the problem, ask: if I reach the same (r,c) in two different ways, can the future be different? If NO -> dp[r][c] is enough. If YES -> add the information causing the difference. Same cell + different obstacles remaining -> dp[r][c][k]." },
      { type: "text", heading: "6-7. Example -- Shortest Path With Obstacle Elimination",
        body: "Start at (0,0), reach (m-1,n-1), move in four directions, may eliminate at most K obstacles. Why isn't normal BFS enough? Suppose you reach (r,c) once after using 3 obstacles, and another time after using only 1. The second is strictly better -- it leaves more power for the future. So visited[r][c] alone is insufficient." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "8. State", dark: true,
        body: "Use (r,c,k) where k = obstacles remaining.\n\nvisited[r][c][k] means: have we already reached (r,c) with k obstacles remaining?\n\nThis is actually BFS + State rather than traditional bottom-up DP -- and that distinction matters." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "9. Why Not Call Everything DP?", tone: "red",
        body: "Movement is UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT, so cycles exist (A -> B -> C -> A). Simple bottom-up DP doesn't work. But the state idea is identical: state = (r,c,k), and BFS explores those states.",
        conclusion: "DP state modeling can be used even when the final algorithm is BFS/Dijkstra rather than DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "10. State-Space Thinking", dark: true,
        body: "Instead of visualizing only the grid, visualize (r,c,k). Every combination is a node: (0,0,2), (0,1,2), (0,2,1), (1,0,1)...\n\nGrid + Constraint -> Expanded State Graph. Transitions connect these states." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "11. Java -- BFS Over (r,c,k)", dark: true,
        body: "boolean[][][] visited = new boolean[m][n][k + 1];\nQueue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();\nqueue.offer(new int[]{0, 0, k});\nvisited[0][0][k] = true;\nint steps = 0;\n\nwhile (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n    int size = queue.size();\n    for (int x = 0; x < size; x++) {\n        int[] cur = queue.poll();\n        int r = cur[0], c = cur[1], remaining = cur[2];\n\n        if (r == m - 1 && c == n - 1) return steps;\n\n        for (int[] dir : DIRS) {\n            int nr = r + dir[0], nc = c + dir[1];\n            if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n) continue;\n\n            int nextRemaining = remaining - grid[nr][nc];\n            if (nextRemaining < 0) continue;\n\n            if (!visited[nr][nc][nextRemaining]) {\n                visited[nr][nc][nextRemaining] = true;\n                queue.offer(new int[]{nr, nc, nextRemaining});\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    steps++;\n}\nreturn -1;" },
      { type: "callout", heading: "12-13. Dominance", tone: "gold",
        body: "Suppose you reach (r,c) with 5 obstacles remaining, and later with 3 remaining. The second state is dominated -- 5 remaining is always at least as good as 3 for future movement. So A=(r,c,5) dominates B=(r,c,3); discard B. Instead of visited[r][c][k] you can sometimes store maxRemaining[r][c] and prune any state where remaining <= maxRemaining[r][c].",
        conclusion: "Dominance shows up in BFS+state, Dijkstra+state, DP, and resource-constrained path problems generally." },
      { type: "text", heading: "14-16. Energy, Exact Sum, and Counting Paths With Exact Sum",
        body: "Remaining energy: state (r,c,energy) -- BFS if minimizing steps, DP if the grid is directional/acyclic. Exact sum: dp[r][c][sum] = whether we can reach (r,c) with total sum = sum, via dp[r][c][sum] = dp[r-1][c][sum-grid[r][c]] || dp[r][c-1][sum-grid[r][c]] (genuine DP if movement is directional). Counting paths with exact sum reuses the identical state, just with += instead of ||: same state, different aggregation." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "17-18. Teleport Once", dark: true,
        body: "'You may use one teleport.' (r,c) is insufficient because (r,c, teleport unused) and (r,c, teleport used) have different futures. State: dp[r][c][used] where used in {0,1}.\n\nFrom (r,c,0): normal move, or teleport -> (newR,newC,1).\nFrom (r,c,1): only normal moves allowed.\n\nThis is State Machine thinking fused with Grid DP." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "19-22. Grid + Bitmask", dark: true,
        body: "'Collect all keys in the grid.' Four keys A B C D -> represent collected keys as bits: 0000 initially, 0001 after A, 0101 after A+C. State becomes (r,c,mask).\n\nReaching (r,c) with mask=0011 vs mask=1111 are very different situations -- the second has strictly more keys and different future possibilities. So visited[r][c] is insufficient; need visited[r][c][mask].\n\nState size: O(m x n x 2^K) -- this is why bitmask problems usually need K <= ~15-20, not hundreds." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "23-24. Grid + Time / Parity", tone: "red",
        body: "'You can only enter certain cells at certain times' suggests (r,c,time) -- but if time can be huge (e.g. 1e9), dp[m][n][time] is impossible. Look for periodicity, modulo, parity, or a mathematical invariant instead. Example: 'reach the destination in exactly K moves' or 'a cell can only be entered on even/odd time' often only needs time % 2. State: (r,c,parity) instead of (r,c,time).",
        conclusion: "A beautiful example of state compression." },
      { type: "text", heading: "25-26. Grid + Modulo & the General State Compression Principle",
        body: "'Find paths whose sum is divisible by K' -- you don't need the full sum, only sum % K. State: dp[r][c][remainder], often dramatically smaller than dp[r][c][sum]. The general principle: ask what exact information about the past affects the future. Maybe you only need parity, remainder, used/not-used, remaining count, min/max resource, or a bitmask -- never the raw full value. This can turn an impossible DP into a practical one." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "27. Constraint Grid DP vs State Machine DP", items: [
        { label: "State Machine DP", body: "Extra dimension represents a mode/action state, e.g. holding, not holding, cooldown." },
        { label: "Constraint Grid DP", body: "Extra dimension represents a resource/constraint/history, e.g. obstacles remaining, energy, sum, keys." }
      ], conclusion: "Structurally both are dp[position][state] -- the same underlying idea wearing different labels." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "28-29. Constraint Grid DP vs BFS+State vs Dijkstra+State", items: [
        { label: "DP", body: "Movement RIGHT/DOWN (acyclic) + objective is min/max/count -> DP candidate." },
        { label: "BFS + State", body: "4-direction movement + objective is shortest number of steps -> same state modeling, different traversal algorithm." },
        { label: "Dijkstra + State", body: "4-direction movement + different move costs + a constraint like K obstacle eliminations -> state (r,c,k), solved via Dijkstra because it's weighted shortest path." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "30. Constraint Grid DP vs Greedy", tone: "red",
        body: "Given (r,c,k) and a constraint, don't assume 'always take the cheapest next cell' is correct. A locally cheap choice might consume a resource and block the destination entirely. When future possibilities depend on resource usage, state usually matters more than local greed." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "31. The State Design Process",
        intro: "When solving a constrained grid problem, work through these steps in order:",
        items: [
          "Step 1 -- Position: start from (r,c).",
          "Step 2 -- Ask what past information affects future decisions (maybe k, maybe more).",
          "Step 3 -- Add it: (r,c,k).",
          "Step 4 -- Define the state precisely, e.g. 'dp[r][c][k] = minimum cost to reach (r,c) with k obstacles remaining.'",
          "Step 5 -- Determine transitions: (r,c,k) -> (nr,nc,newK).",
          "Step 6 -- Choose the algorithm: acyclic + optimization/count -> DP; unweighted shortest path -> BFS; weighted shortest path -> Dijkstra."
        ]},
      { type: "variants", heading: "32-35. Common Mistakes", items: [
        { id: "1", title: "Adding unnecessary dimensions", note: "For 'you may use a coupon once,' don't build dp[r][c][couponCount][day][cost][...]. Ask what actually changes future possibilities -- usually just couponUsed, so dp[r][c][2] is enough." },
        { id: "2", title: "Tracking full history", note: "Storing the entire path taken is usually impossible. Ask for the minimum summary of history that matters: full key set -> bitmask, full sum -> sum % K, full time -> time % period." },
        { id: "3", title: "Ignoring dominance", note: "(r,c,10 energy) and (r,c,5 energy) both existing is wasteful if more energy is always better -- discard the dominated state. This can turn theoretical O(mnK) into something practically fast." },
        { id: "4", title: "Using DP where cycles exist", note: "UP/DOWN/LEFT/RIGHT movement plus a naive dp[r][c] can create circular dependencies. Recognize it as a graph problem instead of forcing DP." }
      ]},
      { type: "callout", heading: "36. Interview Explanation Template", tone: "gold",
        body: "Interviewer: 'Shortest path with at most K obstacle removals.' Strong answer: 'The cell coordinates alone aren't sufficient because reaching the same cell with different numbers of removals remaining gives different future possibilities. So I'll define the state as (row, col, remainingK). Since every movement has unit cost and movement is allowed in four directions, this becomes BFS over the expanded state graph.'",
        conclusion: "Notice: you explain WHY the state needs three dimensions, not just 'use BFS.'" },
      { type: "text", heading: "37. Another Example -- Exact K Turns",
        body: "'Move through the grid and make exactly K turns.' Position alone isn't enough -- you need something like (r,c,turns,direction), because whether the next move creates a turn depends on the previous direction. So position + turn count + previous direction becomes the state. Multiple constraints can stack this way." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "38-40. State Explosion & Complexity Formulas", tone: "red",
        body: "Grid 100x100 with K=100 gives 100*100*100 = 1,000,000 states -- maybe manageable. But mask = 20 bits gives 100*100*2^20, which is enormous. Always estimate 'number of states x transitions per state' before committing to a solution.\n\ndp[r][c][k]: states = rows*cols*K, time = O(rows*cols*K*4) which simplifies to O(rows*cols*K).\n\ndp[r][c][mask] with K keys: states = rows*cols*2^K, time = O(rows*cols*2^K) with four neighbors.",
        conclusion: "This capacity estimation is very useful in interviews -- it immediately tells you whether an approach is even feasible." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "41-42. The Unified State Formula", dark: true,
        body: "Basic:              dp[r][c]\nConstraint:          dp[r][c][state]\nMore constraints:    dp[r][c][state1][state2]\n\n1D:            dp[i]\nState Machine: dp[i][state]\nPrefix:        dp[i], j = transition boundary\nGrid:          dp[r][c]\nConstraint Grid: dp[r][c][state]\nKnapsack:      dp[i][capacity]\nLCS:           dp[i][j]\nBitmask DP:    dp[mask][i]\nDigit DP:      dp[position][tight][started][...]\n\nAll of them: State = the minimum information needed to describe everything relevant about the past. Don't add dimensions blindly -- the real question is always what information is necessary to determine future transitions." },
      { type: "practice", heading: "43. Your Recognition Framework",
        intro: "Don't ask 'which LeetCode pattern is this?' Instead ask, in order:",
        items: [
          "What is changing?",
          "What information from the past affects the future?",
          "Can I summarize that information?",
          "What are the transitions?",
          "Are the states acyclic?",
          "What is the objective?"
        ],
        note: "Then select DP, BFS, Dijkstra, Greedy, or DFS. This is how you move beyond memorizing patterns." },
      { type: "comparison", heading: "44. Constraint Grid Cheat Sheet", items: [
        { label: "K obstacles", body: "k" },
        { label: "Exact sum", body: "sum" },
        { label: "Sum divisible by K", body: "sum % K" },
        { label: "Energy", body: "energy" },
        { label: "Teleport once / coupon", body: "used" },
        { label: "Keys", body: "mask" },
        { label: "Time parity", body: "time % 2" },
        { label: "Exact turns", body: "turns + direction" },
        { label: "Remaining moves", body: "moves" }
      ]},
      { type: "diagram", heading: "45. Algorithm Decision Cheat Sheet", dark: true,
        body: "Grid\n |\n +-- Directional / acyclic -> DP\n |\n +-- 4-direction / cycles\n         |\n         +-- Equal cost -> BFS\n         |\n         +-- Weighted -> Dijkstra\n\nAdd constraints: (r,c) -> (r,c,state), then apply the appropriate algorithm." },
      { type: "callout", heading: "46. The Most Important Interview Insight", tone: "gold",
        body: "A Staff/Senior interviewer isn't impressed that you know 'this is LeetCode 1293.' They care whether you can reason: 'Position alone isn't sufficient. The number of obstacles remaining changes future reachability, so I'll expand the state to (r,c,k). Since transitions have equal cost, I'll perform BFS over this state space.'",
        conclusion: "That is pattern mastery." },
      { type: "diagram", heading: "47. Tier 6 -- Grid Family Complete", dark: true,
        body: "59. 2D Grid DP         done\n60. Grid Path DP       done\n61. Constraint Grid DP done\n\nGrid\n |\n +-- Directional\n |     +-- Count -> DP\n |     +-- Min   -> DP\n |     +-- Max   -> DP\n |     +-- Bool  -> DP\n |\n +-- Constraint\n       +-- extra resource -> state\n       +-- keys           -> bitmask\n       +-- parity         -> modulo state\n       +-- movement cycles\n              +-- unweighted -> BFS\n              +-- weighted   -> Dijkstra" },
      { type: "checklist", heading: "Tier 6 progress",
        items: [{ label: "54. 1D DP", done: true }, { label: "55. Take / Skip DP", done: true },
          { label: "56. Linear DP", done: true }, { label: "57. State Machine DP", done: true },
          { label: "58. Prefix DP", done: true }, { label: "59. 2D Grid DP", done: true },
          { label: "60. Grid Path DP", done: true }, { label: "61. Constraint Grid DP", done: true },
          { label: "62. 0/1 Knapsack", done: false }, { label: "63. Unbounded Knapsack", done: false },
          { label: "64. Subset Sum", done: false }, { label: "65. Partition DP", done: false },
          { label: "66. Target Sum", done: false }, { label: "67. Coin Change", done: false },
          { label: "68. LCS", done: false }, { label: "69. LIS", done: false },
          { label: "70. Edit Distance", done: false }, { label: "71. Palindrome DP", done: false },
          { label: "72. String DP", done: false }, { label: "73. Interval DP", done: false },
          { label: "74. Matrix Chain", done: false }, { label: "75. Burst Balloons", done: false },
          { label: "76. Stock DP", done: false }, { label: "77. Counting DP", done: false },
          { label: "78. Tree DP", done: false }, { label: "79. DAG DP", done: false },
          { label: "80. Bitmask DP", done: false }, { label: "81. Digit DP", done: false },
          { label: "82. DP + Binary Search", done: false }, { label: "83. DP + Monotonic Queue", done: false }] },
      { type: "nextLesson", pattern: "0/1 Knapsack",
        points: ["Items + limited capacity + each item usable ONCE -- deriving why the state becomes dp[i][capacity]", "Why 0/1 Knapsack is one of the highest-priority DP families for interviews", "Connecting 0/1 Knapsack -> Subset Sum -> Partition -> Target Sum -> Coin Change as variations of one underlying pattern"] }
    ]
  },
};
